  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Under a random-effects model, the simple one-way ANOVA model becomes $ y_{ij} = \\mu + t_i + e_{ij} $, where $ t $ is now a random variable drawn from a normal distribution with variance $ \\sigma_t^2 $, so that $ t_i \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0, \\sigma_t^2) $. Residuals are assumed independent with $ e_{ij} \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0, \\sigma_e^2) $ as before. Assuming a balanced design with $ n $ replicates for each of the $ N $ $ t_i $ values, the treatment and error sums of squares are also given by Equation A5.18a. As with the fixed-effects model, $ \\mathrm{SS}_e \\sim \\sigma_e^2 \\cdot \\chi^2_{N(n-1)} $. However, the distribution of the treatment sum of squares remains a central $ \\chi^2 $, even when $ \\sigma_t^2 > 0 $. To see this, note that $ \\overline{y}_i \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0, \\sigma^2) $ with $ \\sigma^2 = \\sigma_t^2 + \\sigma_e^2 / n $. Applying Equation A5.14c, the same logic leading to Equation A5.19 now gives",
